course: dictionary
lesson: 7253
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The words around pierce are the clues. «The needle pierced the cloth.» A needle has a sharp point, so pierce means to make a hole through something.
explain: «She had her ears pierced.» Here pierce means a hole is made in a part of the body so that jewellery can be worn. The clue is ears, a part of the body, not cloth.
explain: «A scream pierced the silence.» A scream cannot make a hole, so pierce must mean something else. It means a sudden sound goes right through the quiet. Always try the meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Pierce is a verb: pierce, pierces, pierced, piercing.
fact: «Pierce» means to make a hole in or through something with a sharp point.
fact: «Piercing» can be an adjective meaning very loud and high, very sharp, or very cold, as in «piercing eyes».
fact: «Pierced» can mean a hole has been made in a body part so that jewellery can be worn.
fact: Lithium batteries hold a lot of energy. If one swells, gets hot or smells, tell an adult, and never pierce or crush it.
exercise: «The needle pierced the cloth.» What does pierced mean? | *Made a hole through with a sharp point | Folded neatly | Dropped on the floor | Washed clean
exercise: «A scream pierced the silence.» What does pierced mean here? | *Suddenly went through the quiet | Repeated softly | Made a hole in the silence | Stopped the silence forever
exercise: In «never pierce or crush it», what should you never do to a battery? | Put it in the sun | Name it | *Make a hole in it with something sharp | Charge it overnight
test: «She had her ears pierced.» In this sentence, pierced means | Cleaned gently | Drawn carefully | *Had a hole made so she can wear jewellery | Heard loudly
test: «Piercing eyes» suggests eyes that are | *Very sharp, seeming to look right through you | Coloured bright blue | Soft and kind | Sleepy and tired
exam: Which sentence uses pierce to mean a sudden sound going through the air? | *«A scream pierced the silence.» | «She had her ears pierced.» | «The needle pierced the cloth.»
